                    },
                    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
                    "body": "I like to have the consistency of opening a lot of my sessions to points that I'm expecting in order to have a consistent experience. Like my nvim config session, I like to open to the start of my main `init.lua` with nothing else open. And there are multiple times where I've jumped into session X just to make a quick edit and jump back to session Y but didn't want to have X's session changed.\r\n\r\nCurrently, I find myself having to `:qa` and re-open nvim to avoid the layout changing, and in my case it's happening more often than I save the session intentionally. It might just be because that's how the first session manager I used did things, so I got used to the workflow.",
                    "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">I like to have the consistency of opening a lot of my sessions to points that I'm expecting in order to have a consistent experience. Like my nvim config session, I like to open to the start of my main <code class=\"notranslate\">init.lua</code> with nothing else open. And there are multiple times where I've jumped into session X just to make a quick edit and jump back to session Y but didn't want to have X's session changed.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Currently, I find myself having to <code class=\"notranslate\">:qa</code> and re-open nvim to avoid the layout changing, and in my case it's happening more often than I save the session intentionally. It might just be because that's how the first session manager I used did things, so I got used to the workflow.</p>",
                    "bodyText": "I like to have the consistency of opening a lot of my sessions to points that I'm expecting in order to have a consistent experience. Like my nvim config session, I like to open to the start of my main init.lua with nothing else open. And there are multiple times where I've jumped into session X just to make a quick edit and jump back to session Y but didn't want to have X's session changed.\nCurrently, I find myself having to :qa and re-open nvim to avoid the layout changing, and in my case it's happening more often than I save the session intentionally. It might just be because that's how the first session manager I used did things, so I got used to the workflow.",
