                    "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
                    "body": "> I like to have the consistency of opening a lot of my sessions to points that I'm expecting in order to have a consistent experience. Like my nvim config session, I like to open to the start of my main `init.lua` with nothing else open. \r\n> ...\r\n> Currently, I find myself having to `:qa` and re-open nvim to avoid the layout changing, and in my case it's happening more often than I save the session intentionally. It might just be because that's how the first session manager I used did things, so I got used to the workflow.\r\n\r\nSo as far as I understand, you have `MiniSessions.config.autowrite` set to `false`, correct? Otherwise, it would have saved the current state of the session (i.e. not only the 'init.lua' file, but also some other files opened just before `:qa`).\r\n",
                    "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I like to have the consistency of opening a lot of my sessions to points that I'm expecting in order to have a consistent experience. Like my nvim config session, I like to open to the start of my main <code class=\"notranslate\">init.lua</code> with nothing else open.<br>\n...<br>\nCurrently, I find myself having to <code class=\"notranslate\">:qa</code> and re-open nvim to avoid the layout changing, and in my case it's happening more often than I save the session intentionally. It might just be because that's how the first session manager I used did things, so I got used to the workflow.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">So as far as I understand, you have <code class=\"notranslate\">MiniSessions.config.autowrite</code> set to <code class=\"notranslate\">false</code>, correct? Otherwise, it would have saved the current state of the session (i.e. not only the 'init.lua' file, but also some other files opened just before <code class=\"notranslate\">:qa</code>).</p>",
                    "bodyText": "I like to have the consistency of opening a lot of my sessions to points that I'm expecting in order to have a consistent experience. Like my nvim config session, I like to open to the start of my main init.lua with nothing else open.\n...\nCurrently, I find myself having to :qa and re-open nvim to avoid the layout changing, and in my case it's happening more often than I save the session intentionally. It might just be because that's how the first session manager I used did things, so I got used to the workflow.\n\nSo as far as I understand, you have MiniSessions.config.autowrite set to false, correct? Otherwise, it would have saved the current state of the session (i.e. not only the 'init.lua' file, but also some other files opened just before :qa).",
